                <pb facs="0567" n="463" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg463"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01 Manchuria/15: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-28T13:00:00+08:00">February 28, 1932—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 28—12:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>283. Following from American Consul General at Mukden: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“February 27, 4 p.m. Reliably informed Pu Yi will be installed as
                            Provisional Executive of the new state at Changchun on February 29 and
                            that Declaration of Independence from China will be issued at that time
                            and transmitted to foreign governments. For several months. Pu has been
                            under Japanese protection in the leased territory.</p>
                        <p>A 3 days’ demonstration in support of the formation of the state,
                            organized by the municipal authorities, is being held at Mukden
                            beginning today. Demonstration is taking the form of free theatres, <pb
                                facs="0568" n="464" xml:id="pg464"/> dragon parades, and meetings of
                            representatives of the districts. Printed pamphlets are being widely
                            distributed. No enthusiasm is noticeable.”</p></quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
